1. An automotive laser headlamp comprising:  
at least one laser light source configured to emit a primary laser beam;  
at least one light guide optically coupled to the at least one laser light source and configured to guide the primary laser beam from an input end to an output end, the at least one light guide being arranged to modify an intensity distribution of the primary laser beam such that the primary laser beam emerges from the output end with an altered intensity profile relative to an intensity profile at the input end;  
a scanning device arranged to receive the primary laser beam emerging from the output end of the at least one light guide and to scan the primary laser beam in a predetermined scanning pattern;  
a light conversion means positioned to be impinged by the scanned primary laser beam and configured to convert at least a portion of the primary laser beam into converted light having a wavelength different from that of the primary laser beam; and  
an imaging optical system configured to project light emitted from the light conversion means as an image onto a road surface in front of a vehicle.

1. **A motor vehicle laser illumination apparatus**, comprising:

   a plurality of laser diodes configured to emit respective laser beams;

   a plurality of beam-coupling light guides, each beam-coupling light guide being optically coupled to at least one of the plurality of laser diodes and configured to receive and guide a corresponding laser beam;

   a light conversion means disposed downstream of the beam-coupling light guides and configured to receive laser radiation from the plurality of laser diodes and convert at least a portion of the laser radiation into wavelength-converted light;

   a microscanner arranged in an optical path of light emitted from the light conversion means and configured to steer the light in at least one scanning direction to form a scanned light distribution; and

   projection optics disposed downstream of the microscanner and configured to project the scanned light distribution onto a roadway region in front of the motor vehicle,

   wherein the plurality of laser diodes are independently controllable such that an intensity and/or spatial distribution of the projected light distribution is dynamically adjustable during operation of the motor vehicle.
